g) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 validation campaign] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 ROCm serving runtime]\n    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 documentation]\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 PyTorch ROCm training] --> E005\n    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota] --> E005\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Campaign go/no-go determines the model scope of the productized runtime (HLR-2 names it); true gate, action=rewrite (rescope to cleared models; full no-go descopes productization) |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | Docs must state the validated model list, which is the campaign's deliverable |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the productized runtime |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs describe training support |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Docs describe quota handling |\n| (no edge) E001 ∥ E003/E004 | Rule 14 | Different components/repos; the campaign validates vLLM serving only — training enablement proceeds independently |\n| (no edge) E003 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/15 | Training Operator and Kueue are separate repos with no shared build artifact; the runtime integration is verified by each epic's acceptance criteria |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003, E004 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 |\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- Missing strategy sections: ... teams inferred from architecture-context owning teams.\n- Hardware availability precondition...\n- Upstream ROCm image cadence...\n- KServe passive classification note (component mapping).\n- Docs priority: HLR-4 P2 → epic P1 per docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\n- External dependency: AMD-controlled ROCm runtime image cadence — third party resolves; modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery, no separate epic (Rule 21).\n- Tier 1 (independent value): E001 campaign (risk-reduction data), E003/E004 training enablement.\n- Tier 2 (gated): E002 productization (campaign go/no-go + pinned upstream ROCm image), E005 docs.\n\nAlso a \"Component Mapping Notes\" section maybe within health warnings or separate — Step 2 says \"Log any non-obvious mappings in the decomposition summary\". I'll add a section \"Component Mapping Notes\" covering: KServe passive; all four components in architecture context (no onboarding chains); E001 component choice.\n\nFrontmatter: parent_strat, epic_count=5, critical_path_length=3.\n\nNow epic bodies.\n\n=== E001 ===\n\nTitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n\n## Description\nRuns a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators to establish functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the platform's supported model set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models the productized ROCm serving runtime will support.\n\n## Scope\n- Define the campaign protocol: enumerate the supported model set, define per-model functional pass/fail criteria, and define the performance parity measurement methodology (throughput and latency on AMD Instinct vs the productized CUDA baseline).\n- Establish the hardware access arrangement required to execute the campaign (procurement of dedicated AMD Instinct nodes or a partner-lab engagement) — the open question of access is unresolved in the strategy.\n- Execute the campaign: serve each supported model with vLLM ROCm builds on AMD Instinct hardware, collecting functional results and performance measurements.\n- Produce the validation report with per-model results and a go/no-go recommendation stating the cleared model scope for productization, including root-cause notes for models that fail or underperform.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The validation report covers every model in the supported model set, with a recorded functional pass/fail result and performance measurements against the CUDA baseline for each.\n- The report states a go/no-go recommendation with an explicit list of cleared models, traceable to the per-model results.\n- Models that fail functional validation or miss performance parity are documented with enough root-cause detail to scope any follow-up work.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): the validation campaign and its go/no-go scope are this epic's entire deliverable.\n\nRisks/Notes: hardware access is an availability precondition controlled by procurement/partner parties — not an AC; campaign start is exposed to their timing.\n\nWait — I should include a Risks section? \"minimum — add additional sections when the strategy contains relevant content for this epic's scope, e.g., risks, assumptions, open questions\". Yes, add Risks/Notes for hardware access and upstream image cadence (the campaign uses upstream ROCm builds whose cadence is AMD-controlled).\n\n=== E002 ===\n\nTitle: Productize a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime\n\n## Description\nProductizes a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models cleared by the validation campaign, shipped through the existing runtime productization pipeline alongside the CUDA builds.\n\n## Scope\n- Build and release a vLLM ROCm runtime image for the platform version, mirroring the existing CUDA runtime image productization in the midstream vLLM repo.\n- Ship a ServingRuntime/ClusterServingRuntime definition that deploys the ROCm runtime with AMD GPU resource requests so KServe InferenceServices can use it. (KServe itself requires no code changes — accelerator-agnostic; its scheduling of InferenceServices onto AMD nodes is validated here.)\n- Pin an upstream ROCm base image release that is compatible with the platform release train.\n- Verify end-to-end serving on AMD Instinct nodes for the cleared model set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The vLLM ROCm runtime image builds green in the platform build pipeline and is released for the platform version.\n- A ServingRuntime definition deploying the ROCm runtime is available, and KServe InferenceServices using it schedule onto AMD Instinct nodes and serve inference requests for the cleared model set.\n- The runtime pins a released upstream ROCm base image compatible with the platform release.\n- Existing CUDA-based runtimes and ServingRuntimes are unaffected (no regression).\n- Rollback: hmm, Rule 23 — strategy replaces existing capability? No. Skip.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P0): the productized supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime.\n\nNote scope depends on campaign outcome — in frontmatter gated_by. In body, describe in plain terms: \"for the model set cleared by the validation campaign\" — allowed (plain terms, not epic IDs).\n\n=== E003 ===\n\nTitle: PyTorch ROCm training support in the Training Operator\n\n## Description\nEnables PyTorch training on ROCm for single- and multi-GPU jobs run through the Training Operator, so PyTorchJob workloads can execute on AMD Instinct nodes.\n\n## Scope\n- Enable PyTorchJob execution on ROCm-enabled nodes: PyTorch ROCm training images and job specifications that request AMD GPU resources for single-GPU and multi-GPU (distributed) topologies.\n- Validate functional training runs on AMD Instinct hardware for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Single-GPU PyTorchJob workloads requesting AMD GPU resources run to completion on ROCm nodes with correct training results.\n- Multi-GPU distributed PyTorchJob workloads requesting AMD GPU resources run to completion on ROCm nodes.\n- Existing NVIDIA-based PyTorchJob behavior is unaffected.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-3 (P1) (training execution half; queue admission and quota covered by the Kueue quota epic).\n\nHmm — \"No cross-references to sibling epics\" — saying \"the Kueue quota epic\" is a plain-terms description, not an ID. That's allowed (\"describe what it delivers and depends on in plain terms\"). OK.\n\nRisks: validation requires physical AMD Instinct hardware (shared availability risk with the serving campaign).\n\n=== E004 ===\n\nTitle: Kueue quota handling for AMD GPU (amd.com/gpu) resources\n\nTitle must avoid enforcement words — \"quota handling\" fine.\n\n## Description\nExtends the Distributed Workloads queueing layer so workloads requesting AMD GPU resources (amd.com/gpu) are admitted through Kueue with correct quota handling, covering the admission path for ROCm training jobs.\n\n## Scope\n- Support amd.com/gpu in Kueue quota: ClusterQueue/LocalQueue resource flavors covering AMD GPU resources so workloads requesting them are admitted under quota with all-or-nothing (gang) semantics.\n- Validate quota accounting and admission for multi-GPU PyTorchJob workloads requesting amd.com/gpu.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Workloads requesting amd.com/gpu are admitted through Kueue quota (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue) and run to completion, including multi-GPU PyTorchJob topologies.\n- Quota limits on amd.com/gpu are enforced: a workload exceeding its queue's quota remains pending rather than being partially admitted.\n- Existing NVIDIA GPU quota handling is unaffected.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-3 (P1) (queue admission and amd.com/gpu quota half; PyTorchJob execution covered by the training operator epic).\n\n=== E005 ===\n\nTitle: Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support for serving and training\n\n## Description\nAuthor and publish platform documentation stating the supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list, describing the ROCm serving and training capabilities delivered by this strategy.\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Supported AMD Instinct hardware (models/configurations validated by the campaign).\n- Driver and node prerequisites for AMD GPU nodes.\n- The validated model list for the vLLM ROCm serving runtime, matching the validation campaign results.\n- Using ROCm