solved (strategy open\n  question 1). Access being granted is externally controlled and is NOT an acceptance\n  criterion anywhere; E001's scope includes *securing* the arrangement, and the AC sits\n  on the validated report. The same hardware constraint affects the functional\n  validation ACs on E003 and E004 — shared risk, tracked in each epic's Risks.\n- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (non-blocking).** AMD controls ROCm runtime image\n  release timing (strategy open question 2). Modeled as a precondition with tiered\n  delivery (below), not as a separate epic: the productization epic pins a released\n  upstream ROCm base image compatible with the platform release train. Schedule impact\n  from upstream timing cannot be compressed by this decomposition.\n- **Docs priority note (not an inversion).** HLR-4 is P2, but the docs-authoring epic\n  is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority\n  `Major` → P1). Docs edges do not trigger priority inheritance.\n- No priority inversions detected: P0 serving validation/productization ahead of P1\n  training enablement is consistent with the strategy's customer-driven emphasis.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (AMD-controlled upstream ROCm image cadence) has uncertain\ntiming and is resolved by a third party — modeled as a precondition with tiered\ndelivery, no separate epic (Rule 21):\n\n- **Tier 1 — independent value (proceed now):** E001 (risk-reduction validation data\n  and the go/no-go scope), E003 and E004 (training enablement, independent of the\n  serving campaign).\n- **Tier 2 — gated:** E002 (productization; gated by the campaign outcome and by the\n  availability of a released upstream ROCm base image compatible with the release\n  train), then E005 (documentation of what shipped).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":43264,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-too