{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbb315ce14fe7f08","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training — Decomposition\n11\t\n12\tStrategy RHAISTRAT-9020 (priority: Major) enables AMD Instinct (ROCm) accelerators\n13\tfor vLLM serving and PyTorch training. All four strategy components (KServe, Serving\n14\tRuntimes, Training Operator, Distributed Workloads) have architecture-context files —\n15\tno onboarding chains are needed. The strategy carries two explicit open questions\n16\t(hardware access, upstream ROCm image cadence), which are handled as noted below.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support in the Training Operator | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue quota handling for AMD GPU (amd.com/gpu) resources | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support for serving and training | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n27\t\n28\t## Dependency DAG\n29\t\n30\t```mermaid\n31\tgraph TD\n32\t    E001[\"E001 validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 ROCm serving runtime productization\"]\n33\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 documentation\"]\n34\t    E002 --> E005\n35\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorch ROCm training\"] --> E005\n36\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E005\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\t## DAG Justification\n40\t\n41\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n42\t|------|------|-----------|\n43\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | HLR-2 scopes the productized runtime to \"the models the validation campaign clears\" — the campaign outcome is a true gate (`rewrite`: rescope to the cleared model set; a full no-go descopes productization). Recorded as `gated_by` on E002. |\n44\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | HLR-4 requires docs to state the validated model list, which is the campaign's direct deliverable. |\n45\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the productized runtime. |\n46\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the delivered training support. |\n47\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the delivered quota handling. |\n48\t| no edge E001 ∥ E003, E004 | Rule 14 (independent repos) | The campaign validates vLLM serving only (HLR-1); the training track (Training Operator, Kueue) proceeds independently in separate repos. |\n49\t| no edge E003 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/15 | Training Operator and Kueue are separate components/repos with no shared build artifact; the runtime integration (PyTorchJobs admitted under amd.com/gpu quota) is verified by each epic's acceptance criteria, not by a DAG edge. |\n50\t\n51\tConditional branches: the campaign's outcomes are bounded (full pass / partial pass /\n52\tno-go) but they change only the model-set scope of the single downstream productization\n53\tepic, not which epics exist or how the DAG is structured — E002 covers \"the models the\n54\tcampaign clears\" under every outcome. The gate is therefore expressed as\n55\t`gate_failure_impact` on E002 rather than as branch epic files.\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n58\t\n59\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n60\t|-----|----------|---------|\n61\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 |\n62\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 |\n63\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E003, RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 |\n64\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 |\n65\t\n66\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-3 spans two components\n67\t(Training Operator, Distributed Workloads) owned by the same team, so it maps to two\n68\tepics per the component-boundary rule — the split is architectural (job execution vs\n69\tqueue admission/quota), not priority-based.\n70\t\n71\t## Component Mapping Notes\n72\t\n73\t- **KServe — passive component.** The strategy lists KServe as affected but describes\n74\t  no change to it, and the architecture context (`kserve.md`) shows KServe as the\n75\t  accelerator-agnostic lifecycle/routing layer over ServingRuntime CRDs. The ROCm work\n76\t  lives in the runtime images and ServingRuntime definitions. KServe validation\n77\t  (InferenceServices scheduling onto AMD Instinct nodes) is an acceptance criterion on\n78\t  E002, the nearest active epic — same owning team (RHOAI Serving), so no separate\n79\t  validation epic is generated.\n80\t- **E001 component.** The campaign validates vLLM serving, so it is filed under the\n81\t  canonical component `Serving Runtimes` (closest canonical name for the vLLM runtime\n82\t  work it gates).\n83\t- **E005 component.** Documentation work is filed under the canonical component\n84\t  `Documentation`.\n85\t- All components are present in `.context/architecture-context/` — no new-component\n86\t  onboarding chains (Rule 20) apply. The ROCm runtime image is a new image variant in\n87\t  the already-Konflux-onboarded vLLM midstream repo, so it is handled inside E002's\n88\t  scope rather than as a separate onboarding epic.\n89\t\n90\t## Health Warnings\n91\t\n92\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n93\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional\n94\t  Requirements. Teams were inferred from the architecture-context owning-team fields\n95\t  (KServe and Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator and Distributed\n96\t  Workloads → Distributed Workloads). Per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from\n97\t  the HLRs.\n98\t- **Hardware availability precondition (non-blocking).** No AMD Instinct nodes exist\n99\t  in the CI fleet, and procurement/partner-lab access is unresolved (strategy open\n100\t  question 1). Access being granted is externally controlled and is NOT an acceptance\n101\t  criterion anywhere; E001's scope includes *securing* the arrangement, and the AC sits\n102\t  on the validated report. The same hardware constraint affects the functional\n103\t  validation ACs on E003 and E004 — shared risk, tracked in each epic's Risks.\n104\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (non-blocking).** AMD controls ROCm runtime image\n105\t  release timing (strategy open question 2). Modeled as a precondition with tiered\n106\t  delivery (below), not as a separate epic: the productization epic pins a released\n107\t  upstream ROCm base image compatible with the platform release train. Schedule impact\n108\t  from upstream timing cannot be compressed by this decomposition.\n109\t- **Docs priority note (not an inversion).** HLR-4 is P2, but the docs-authoring epic\n110\t  is P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority\n111\t  `Major` → P1). Docs edges do not trigger priority inheritance.\n112\t- No priority inversions detected: P0 serving validation/productization ahead of P1\n113\t  training enablement is consistent with the strategy's customer-driven emphasis.\n114\t\n115\t## Tiered Delivery\n116\t\n117\tThe external dependency (AMD-controlled upstream ROCm image cadence) has uncertain\n118\ttiming and is resolved by a third party — modeled as a precondition with tiered\n119\tdelivery, no separate epic (Rule 21):\n120\t\n121\t- **Tier 1 — independent value (proceed now):** E001 (risk-reduction validation data\n122\t  and the go/no-go scope), E003 and E004 (training enablement, independent of the\n123\t  serving campaign).\n124\t- **Tier 2 — gated:** E002 (productization; gated by the campaign outcome and by the\n125\t  availability of a released upstream ROCm base image compatible with the release\n126\t  train), then E005 (documentation of what shipped).\n127\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88146ce14146901d","agent_id":"ab45f5054681bada7","session_id":"cfc5c49c-9bde-4a05-b3d1-1a240781a747","uuid":"443398c6-9867-4f17-ac64-9b3dc31276d9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:30:13.773Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbb315ce14fe7f08","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
