{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba6274e10357d3fb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tinvestigation_signals:\n10\t  question_specificity: 1\n11\t  source_accessibility: 0\n12\t  local_runnability: 0\n13\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n14\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n15\timplementation_type: null\n16\tdependencies: []\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tRuns a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators to establish\n30\tfunctional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the platform's supported\n31\tmodel set, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models the\n32\tproductized ROCm serving runtime will support. This resolves the strategy's core\n33\tunknown — the platform ships only CUDA runtime builds and has no first-hand ROCm\n34\tparity data for its supported models.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Define the campaign protocol: enumerate the supported model set, define per-model\n39\t  functional pass/fail criteria, and define the performance parity measurement\n40\t  methodology (throughput and latency on AMD Instinct versus the productized CUDA\n41\t  baseline).\n42\t- Establish the hardware access arrangement required to execute the campaign —\n43\t  procurement of dedicated AMD Instinct nodes or a partner-lab engagement (the\n44\t  strategy flags this access as unresolved).\n45\t- Execute the campaign: serve each supported model with vLLM ROCm builds on AMD\n46\t  Instinct hardware, collecting functional results and performance measurements.\n47\t- Produce the validation report: per-model functional and performance results, a\n48\t  go/no-go recommendation stating the cleared model scope for productization, and\n49\t  root-cause notes for models that fail or underperform.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- The validation report covers every model in the supported model set, recording a\n54\t  functional pass/fail result and performance measurements against the CUDA baseline\n55\t  for each model.\n56\t- The report states a go/no-go recommendation with an explicit list of cleared\n57\t  models, traceable to the per-model results.\n58\t- Models that fail functional validation or miss performance parity are documented\n59\t  with root-cause detail sufficient to scope any follow-up work.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- HLR-1 (P0): the validation campaign and the go/no-go scope for productization are\n64\t  this epic's entire deliverable.\n65\t\n66\t## Risks and Notes\n67\t\n68\t- Hardware access is an availability precondition controlled by parties outside the\n69\t  strategy (procurement, partner labs); no AMD Instinct nodes exist in the CI fleet\n70\t  today. The epic's work includes securing the arrangement, but the granting of\n71\t  access and its turnaround are not conditions this epic's deliverable produces —\n72\t  campaign start is exposed to their timing.\n73\t- The campaign consumes upstream vLLM ROCm builds whose release cadence is\n74\t  controlled by AMD; the campaign protocol should pin image versions explicitly.\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88146ce14146901d","agent_id":"ab45f5054681bada7","session_id":"cfc5c49c-9bde-4a05-b3d1-1a240781a747","uuid":"89d0632a-c760-44c0-9f3d-721a0be82677","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:30:15.915Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba6274e10357d3fb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude