{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a7830c1b34ca85a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E002\n3\ttitle: Productize a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: Rescope to the model subset the validation campaign clears; a\n15\t    full no-go descopes the runtime productization entirely and the strategy records\n16\t    ROCm serving as unsupported pending upstream/hardware changes.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 1\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: 0\n23\t  external_dependency: -1\n24\t  human_process_gates: 0\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 4\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Productize a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tProductizes a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models cleared by the AMD\n39\tInstinct validation campaign, built and released through the existing runtime\n40\tproductization pipeline alongside the CUDA builds. The scope of supported models is\n41\tset by the campaign's go/no-go outcome; the engineering approach mirrors the existing\n42\tCUDA vLLM runtime productization.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- Build and release a vLLM ROCm runtime image for the platform version in the\n47\t  midstream vLLM repo, mirroring the existing CUDA runtime image productization.\n48\t- Ship a ServingRuntime/ClusterServingRuntime definition that deploys the ROCm\n49\t  runtime with AMD GPU resource requests so KServe InferenceServices can use it.\n50\t  KServe itself requires no code changes — it is accelerator-agnostic — so\n51\t  validating that InferenceServices schedule and serve correctly on AMD Instinct\n52\t  nodes is part of this epic.\n53\t- Pin an upstream ROCm base image release that is compatible with the platform\n54\t  release train, and record the compatibility choice.\n55\t- Verify end-to-end serving on AMD Instinct nodes for the cleared model set.\n56\t\n57\t## Acceptance Criteria\n58\t\n59\t- The vLLM ROCm runtime image builds green in the platform build pipeline and is\n60\t  released for the platform version.\n61\t- A ServingRuntime definition deploying the ROCm runtime is available, and KServe\n62\t  InferenceServices using it schedule onto AMD Instinct nodes and serve inference\n63\t  requests for every model cleared by the validation campaign.\n64\t- The runtime pins a released upstream ROCm base image compatible with the platform\n65\t  release, and the pinning is recorded.\n66\t- Existing CUDA-based runtime images and ServingRuntime definitions are unaffected\n67\t  (no regression to the currently supported serving paths).\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- HLR-2 (P0): a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for the models the\n72\t  validation campaign clears.\n73\t\n74\t## Risks and Notes\n75\t\n76\t- Upstream ROCm image release cadence is controlled by AMD and may not align with\n77\t  the platform release trains; the mitigation is pinning whichever released upstream\n78\t  image is compatible, and re-pinning per release. This timing risk is externally\n79\t  controlled and cannot be compressed.\n80\t- The model scope of this epic is determined by the validation campaign outcome; a\n81\t  partial pass rescopes the epic to the cleared subset, and a full no-go descopes\n82\t  the productization work entirely.\n83\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88146ce14146901d","agent_id":"ab45f50546