{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85a3200891b7a3eb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tContract validator: OK (exit 0, no violations). Triage check: `triage` is null (full\n15\tdecomposition) with 5 epics — consistent, no triage contradiction. No conditional branch\n16\tfiles exist, and the summary justifies their omission (see Criterion 7).\n17\t\n18\tThe decomposition cleanly translates the four HLRs into a five-epic DAG: a P0\n19\tInvestigation (validation campaign) gating the P0 productization epic, two independent\n20\tP1 training-track epics split by component, and a docs-authoring epic correctly ordered\n21\tlast. The two strategy open questions (hardware access, upstream ROCm cadence) are both\n22\tcarried forward rather than silently dropped. No critical or major issues found; no\n23\tminor issues found.\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\tAll four HLRs map to at least one epic: HLR-1 (P0) → E001, HLR-2 (P0) → E002, HLR-3\n29\t(P1) → E003 + E004, HLR-4 (P2) → E005. The traceability matrix is present and matches\n30\tthe epic files' HLR Traceability sections. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0)\n31\tblocks E002 (P0); no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. E005 is docs-authoring and\n32\tderives its P1 from the strategy's Major priority per the exemption — not an\n33\tinversion. No priority collapse: no epic bundles HLRs at multiple priority levels; the\n34\tHLR-3 split into E003/E004 is architectural (job execution vs queue admission/quota\n35\tacross two components), not priority-based.\n36\t\n37\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n38\tDiagram and frontmatter are exactly consistent: all five diagram edges (E001→E002,\n39\tE001→E005, E002→E005, E003→E005, E004→E005) have matching `dependencies` entries in\n40\tthe target epics (E002: [E001]; E005: [E001, E002, E003, E004]), arrows run\n41\tdependency→dependent, and no frontmatter dependency references a nonexistent epic. No\n42\tcycles. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so\n43\tRules 7/8/10 parallelism is moot; the contract validator confirmed this. Rule 11\n44\tdocs-last coverage is complete — every non-docs epic (E002, E003, E004) plus the\n45\tInvestigation E001 (justified by a real content dependency: the validated model list\n46\tis E001's direct deliverable and HLR-4 requires docs to state it) blocks E005. The\n47\tE003 ∥ E004 parallelism is correctly left unserialized (separate components/repos, no\n48\tshared artifact; the PyTorchJob-under-quota integration is jointly verified by both\n49\tepics' acceptance criteria rather than by a DAG edge — the rule-compliant shape for\n50\tintegration coverage without an artifact flow). Critical path length 3 fits the\n51\tM-standard heuristic (3-4) for a 4-component strategy. No data/artifact dependency\n52\tdiscoverable from epic content is missing from the DAG: E002's consumption of the\n53\tcampaign report is captured by the E001→E002 edge, and its KServe validation is an AC\n54\twithin the epic, not a cross-epic dependency.\n55\t\n56\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n57\tEach component/team tuple produces its own epic: (Serving Runtimes, RHOAI Serving) ×2\n58\t(E001 Investigation, E002 Implementation — different types, same tuple, legitimately\n59\tseparate), (Training Operator, Distributed Workloads), (Distributed Workloads,\n60\tDistributed Workloads), (Documentation, RHOAI Documentation). No epic spans multiple\n61\tteams. KServe is handled as a passive component with its validation folded into E002's\n62\tACs — defensible since the architecture context shows it as the accelerator-agnostic\n63\trouting layer and the owning team is the same. No epic appears oversized relative to\n64\tthe ~2-week guideline (E001's wall-clock risk is externally constrained hardware\n65\taccess, not work volume, and it is recorded in Risks). No scope duplication: E003 and\n66\tE004 each touch the multi-GPU PyTorchJob admission story from their own component's\n67\tside (execution vs quota), which is integration coverage, not a rebuilt surface. Code\n68\tand content are properly separated (E005).\n69\t\n70\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n71\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (cleared model set / go/no-go) changes\n72\tE002's scope and E005's content, and both depend on it. E002-E005 are Implementations\n73\tproducing concrete artifacts (runtime image + ServingRuntime definition, training\n74\tenablement, quota support, published docs). Gating metadata is complete and\n75\tconsistent: E002 and E005 both set `gated_by: E001` with `gate_failure_impact`\n76\tpopulating both `action` (rewrite) and a concrete `fallback_approach`, and both\n77\t`gated_by` targets appear in the epics' direct `dependencies` lists. Both epics that\n78\tlist the Investigation in `dependencies` have `gated_by` set. E005's gate is a true\n79\tgate (a no-go rewrites the docs content rather than merely delaying it), not a\n80\tscheduling dependency.\n81\t\n82\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n83\tType-appropriate signal sets are present in every epic's frontmatter:\n84\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) on E001, `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) on\n85\tE002-E005 — no cross-type contamination. All five ai-signals rationale files exist,\n86\tand the rationales match the frontmatter values. E001's signals are consistent with\n87\tthe rubric: `source_accessibility: 0` is correct (the parity data does not exist\n88\tanywhere and must be measured — not a pending human decision), and\n89\t`cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` matches the strategy's explicit hardware\n90\trequirement. The `architecture_claims: 1` scores are calibrated: the strategy's\n91\tCUDA-only productization premise is an architecture claim, the\n92\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/serving-runtimes.md` file covers\n93\tthe component and corroborates the claim (\"only CUDA (NVIDIA) builds are currently\n94\tproductized\"), and the E002/E005 rationales cite it. No arithmetic or threshold\n95\tchecking performed (computed by the pipeline).\n96\t\n97\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n98\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs\n99\tare present: no-regression ACs on E002 (CUDA images/ServingRuntimes unaffected), E003\n100\t(NVIDIA PyTorchJob behavior unaffected), and E004 (nvidia.com/gpu quota unaffected);\n101\ttechnical-review AC on the docs-authoring E005; build-pipeline-green AC on E002. The\n102\tcanonical soft-AC trap — the externally-controlled hardware availability precondition\n103\t— is handled correctly: no AC anywhere conditions on access being granted; it is\n104\trecorded in Risks/Notes on E001, E003, and E004 and in the summary's Health Warnings,\n105\twhile E001's own deliverable (the validated report) carries the AC. No epic body\n106\treferences sibling epics by draft ID (grep confirms E00x strings appear only in\n107\tfrontmatter fields).\n108\t\n109\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n110\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: both P0 HLRs, the P1 training track split across\n111\tits two components, and the P2 docs HLR. The strategy's out-of-scope boundaries\n112\t(non-AMD accelerators, AMD GPU sharing/partitioning) are carried into E005's content\n113\toutline rather than dropped. Both strategy open questions are carried forward:\n114\thardware access into E001's scope and Risks (plus E003/E004 shared-risk notes), and\n115\tupstream ROCm cadence into E002's Risks with tiered delivery in the summary. All four\n116\taffected components are addressed, including the passive KServe component. The\n117\tomission of conditional branch files is justified: the campaign's bounded outcomes\n118\t(full pass / partial / no-go) change only the model-set scope of the single\n119\tdownstream productization epic, which is expressed as `gate_failure_impact` on E002\n120\twith a concrete fallback for each outcome — the rule-compliant alternative to branch\n121\tepics, and the no-go case is handled on E005 as well. The strategy has no\n122\tPrerequisites & Process Gates section, so no docs-support obligation is unmet (a\n123\tdocs-authoring epic exists anyway via HLR-4).\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad33f9dd2cf3bea2","agent_id":"a6b94f036a7ff029b","session_id":"cfc5c49c-9bde-4a05-b3d1-1a240781a747","uuid":"959be9e9-6b5e-4667-a04b-198a2b92815e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:32:18.324Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-85a3200891b7a3eb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
